Question: What retrieval-augmented methods for language models were proposed in 2020 or 2021?

RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) is a retrieval-augmented method for language models proposed in 2020-2021 (Lewis2005 pages 7-9). RAG combines a dense neural retriever with a pre-trained encoder-decoder language model, specifically BART or T5, to augment generation tasks (Lewis2005 pages 7-9).

The architecture incorporates learned retrieval mechanisms that improve performance across multiple NLP tasks (Lewis2005 pages 7-9). A key advantage of RAG is its non-parametric memory design, which enables knowledge updates at test time by simply swapping the document index without retraining (Lewis2005 pages 7-9). This flexibility allows adjustment of retrieved documents at inference time (Lewis2005 pages 7-9).

RAG demonstrates strong empirical performance across diverse knowledge-intensive tasks, including open-domain question answering, FEVER fact verification, and question generation (Lewis2005 pages 7-9). The unified retrieval-based architecture outperforms parametric-only models like T5 and BART (Lewis2005 pages 7-9). The method's effectiveness across multiple NLP applications demonstrates that a single retrieval-augmented architecture can effectively handle various knowledge-intensive tasks (Lewis2005 pages 7-9).

References

1. (Lewis2005 pages 7-9): Lewis, Patrick, et al. "Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks." arXiv preprint arXiv:2005.11401v4, 12 Apr. 2021, arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401.
